==Reactions==
Anastasiades' victory led to celebrations in the nation's capital of [[Nicosia]] and boosted hopes of reaching a financial bailout deal with international banks.<ref name=VOA>{{cite news|title=Conservative Triumphs in Cyprus Presidential Election|date=24 February 2013|url=https://www.voanews.com/a/conservative-triumphs-in-cyprus-presidential-election/1609876.html|access-date=24 February 2013}}</ref> Economist Stelios Platis remarked "I would expect the markets, and business, to welcome Anastasiades's victory because he knows the rules of the game very well."<ref name=Reuters>{{cite news|title=Cypriot conservative romps to presidential victory|date=24 February 2013|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-cyprus-idUSBRE91N00220130224|accessdate=24 February 2013|work=Reuters}}</ref> However, political analyst Alexander White said immediate resolution to Cyprus' debt problems was unlikely.<ref name=Bloomberg /> [[Agence France-Presse]] remarked that Anastasiades represented a stark contrast to the outgoing government and speculated that "his perceived bias towards big business may put him on a collision course with influential and powerful trade unions".<ref name=AFP>{{cite news|title=Bruiser Anastasiades, new Cyprus president|first= Charlie |last=Charalambous|date=24 February 2013|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5hE1ZbB-dyfblUMNbEgbbKFRwTTGQ?docId=CNG.a3574859f4a34e1650835103f511c361.f1|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130411015954/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5hE1ZbB-dyfblUMNbEgbbKFRwTTGQ?docId=CNG.a3574859f4a34e1650835103f511c361.f1|url-status=dead|archive-date=11 April 2013|accessdate=24 February 2013}}</ref> In his concession speech, Malas pledged to support Anastasiades "in actions and politics that we believe are followed for the good of the country".<ref name=Bloomberg>{{cite news|title=Cyprus Picks Anastasiades as President to Fix Crisis-Hit Economy|date=24 February 2013|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/2013-02-24/cyprus-picks-anastasiades-as-president-to-fix-crisis-hit-economy.html|accessdate=24 February 2013|work=Bloomberg|first1=Georgios|last1=Georgiou|first2=Paul|last2=Tugwell}}</ref>

==Aftermath==
{{further|First Anastasiades government}}
Anastasiades was sworn in on 28 February 2013 and assumed power on 1 March.<ref name=Reuters />

